# Ports and adapters

**Topic 04: The hexagon**

After this talk you can draw the dependency arrows of a hexagonal design, write a port as an interface, and explain why Hadur's core cannot import Robocode.

Java skills: packages, interfaces as ports, functional interfaces, exceptions as a boundary, multi-module Maven.

---

# The picture

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph Robocode
        E[Engine]
    end
    subgraph "hadur-robot (adapter)"
        A["hadur2.Hadur"]
        FS["FileProfileStore"]
    end
    subgraph "hadur-core (plain Java 11)"
        G["Guard"]
        C[HadurCore]
        PS(["port: ProfileStore"])
        T(["port: Telemetry"])
    end
    E -- "events, getters" --> A
    A -- BotInput --> G --> C
    C -- BotOrders --> G -- BotOrders --> A
    A -- "setters, execute()" --> E
    PS -. implemented by .-> FS
```

The middle is the **core**. The edge is the **adapter**. The arrows between are **ports**.

---

# Two modules, one direction

Hadur is a Maven project with modules:

| Module | Holds |
|---|---|
| `hadur-core` | the brain, plain Java |
| `hadur-robot` | the adapter `hadur2.Hadur` and `FileProfileStore` |
| `hadur-bench` | headless battles in the real engine |

`hadur-robot` depends on `hadur-core`. The core depends on nothing of Robocode's, so it cannot even compile a line that mentions it. The compiler enforces the direction before any test runs.

*Source: `docs/architecture.md`, "Modules".*

---

# A port is an interface the core owns

The core needs somewhere to send telemetry, and it may not print:

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Telemetry {

    /** A telemetry sink that discards every line. */
    Telemetry NONE = line -> { };

    void emit(String line);
}
```

One method, so a lambda is an adapter: `line -> console.println(line)` in the robot, `telemetry::add` in a test.

*Source: `hadur2.core.port.Telemetry`.*

---

# A bigger port: ProfileStore

```java
public interface ProfileStore {
    byte[] read(String name);
    void write(String name, byte[] bytes);
    void delete(String name);
    List<String> names();
    long bytesUsed();
    long quota();
```

Named blobs and a quota. The Javadoc says it is "deliberately dumb": the core owns the format and the checksums.

Two adapters implement it: `MemoryProfileStore` for tests and the bench, and `FileProfileStore` in the robot, on Robocode's data directory.

*Source: `hadur2.core.port.ProfileStore` (methods shown without their comments).*

---

# Injecting behaviour, not just objects

The guard needs the core's tick, but it does not name the core class:

```java
/** The core's tick; in the robot, {@link HadurCore#tick}. */
private final Function<BotInput, BotOrders> core;
/** Asks the core to drop its transient round state; in the robot, {@link HadurCore#recover}. */
private final Runnable recover;
```

In the robot, the adapter wires it up:

```java
guard = new Guard(core::tick, core::recover, line -> console.println(line));
```

A method reference is an adapter in one token.

---

# Exceptions as a boundary

If the brain throws, the robot must still move. The guard catches everything at the edge:

```java
        } catch (Throwable t) {
            faultsThisRound++;
            recoverOnNextScan = true;
            // ...
            return safeOrders(in, lastEnemyAbsBearing, faultsThisRound);
        }
```

`Throwable`, not `Exception`: even a `StackOverflowError` must not stop the robot. A `null` result counts as a fault too. The fault is logged once per round through the telemetry port.

*Source: `hadur2.core.Guard.tick`.*

---

# Safe orders

When the core fails the guard still has to give orders. It keeps them simple:

- orbit the enemy, side-on to its last bearing
- lock the radar on that bearing, or spin it if unseen
- hold fire, until the third fault in a round, then fire power 1.0

The guard holds almost no state of its own, "so that it cannot itself be the thing that fails". It remembers the enemy's last bearing itself rather than asking a core that has just crashed.

*Source: `Guard` class comment and `safeOrders`.*

---

# Testing the boundary with a test double

A test double is a stand-in for a real collaborator. A lambda that throws is the whole fake:

```java
@Test
@DisplayName("a throwing core gets safe orders, a fault count and one FAULT record")
void throwingCoreGetsSafeOrders() {
    Guard g = guard(in -> { throw new IllegalStateException("boom, with a comma"); });
    BotOrders a = g.tick(input(7, 5));
    BotOrders b = g.tick(input(8, 5));
    assertEquals(2, g.faultsThisRound());
```

No Robocode. No real core. The test runs in milliseconds.

*Source: `GuardTest`.*

---

# The rule that holds it up

The boundary is a test, not a hope:

```java
noClasses().that().resideInAPackage(CORE)
    .should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage("robocode..", "net.sf.robocode..")
    .check(core);
```

That is requirement CORE-1: "The core shall not import any class from `robocode.*`." Topic 05 shows how it is enforced. Lab 04 splits Hadurling into the same two modules.

*Sources: `docs/requirements.md`, `ArchitectureTest`.*
